1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,880
You ask Claude to change some code, and at the end it hands you a summary.

2
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:13,210
The summary reads smoothly, and every line looks like good news.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,120
But one line got quietly glossed over.

4
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,992
Claude Code just added a built-in

5
00:00:20,012 --> 00:00:23,180
plugin called You should know.

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,867
The official post says it scans Claude's output

7
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:29,540
for important information you might miss, to keep you in the loop.

8
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:33,970
Back to the demo. As soon as the summary ends, a note pops up above the prompt.

9
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:38,180
It warns that the automatic retry just added could charge a customer twice.

10
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:41,169
Press 1 for details: a timeout does not mean no charge.

11
00:00:41,189 --> 00:00:44,620
Sometimes only the reply got lost, and a retry charges again.

12
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,860
The usual fix is an idempotency key: one unique ID per payment.

13
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:53,950
Press 2, and the note goes back to the main session, labeled as offered by a side agent.

14
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:57,010
You ask a quick follow-up: does the key ever expire?

15
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:00,480
Claude replies that the side agent is right, and fixes it right away.

16
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:04,860
Done: each payment gets one random key, sent with every retry.

17
00:01:05,010 --> 00:01:08,220
How does it work? The official team explained in the next post.

18
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,760
It's a mod that spins off a side agent

19
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:13,240
to observe Claude's output.

20
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:15,348
Mods were covered in episode one.

21
00:01:15,368 --> 00:01:19,320
The docs' built-in mods table now includes this one, six in total.

22
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,611
The docs say that while Claude works on longer tasks,

23
00:01:23,631 --> 00:01:27,070
a side agent watches your back and leaves a note above the prompt.

24
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:28,667
Note that it is disabled by default,

25
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:31,460
and your organization has to make it available.

26
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:34,020
To turn it on, type the command the docs give.

27
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:39,060
To turn it off, disable it in /plugin. Built-in mods can't be uninstalled.

28
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:42,093
The changelog says it arrived in 2.1.287,

29
00:01:42,113 --> 00:01:45,380
for first-party sessions with telemetry on.

30
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:48,850
How often it speaks up, and whether it ever misfires, the official docs don't say.

31
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:54,610
However polished a summary is, something may go unsaid. A second pair of eyes helps.
